{
  "id": "proj-welded-fire-pit-ring",
  "slug": "welded-fire-pit-ring",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Welded Fire Pit Ring",
  "summary": "A 36 in, 12 in tall steel ring from three hand-bent 16 gauge sheets, with cut-out vents and a flat-bar top rim. A first welding project that stays useful every summer.",
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedHours": 8,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Trim each 12 x 48 in sheet to 37 3/4 in long; three of them make the 113 in circumference of a 36 in ring.",
    "Mark three or four vent slots, about 2 x 5 in, near the bottom edge of each sheet; drill a corner hole with the step bit and cut the slots with a cut-off wheel.",
    "Bend each sheet by hand around a 36 in round form (a plywood disc or a large drum) until it holds the curve.",
    "Clamp the first two sheets edge to edge with the angle magnets, tack weld them every 2 in, then add the third sheet and tack the ring closed.",
    "Measure across the ring in several directions, tap it round where needed, then stitch weld each seam in short 1 in runs, alternating seams so the heat does not warp the sheet.",
    "Bend the flat bar into a hoop around the top edge, tack it every 6 in, and weld the hoop's own ends together.",
    "Grind the welds flush with a flap disc, wipe the whole ring with acetone, and spray two thin coats of high-heat paint.",
    "Cure the paint with a small first fire before any full-size burns."
  ],
